> Haven't you ever lost a very promising
> character because of *one little* slip of the finger?

Many times - e.g. by the ever-popular floating eye. But that is
exactly the point: i like NetHack because it comes down like a ton of
bricks on stupid playing, it makes _not_ getting splattered so much
more of an achievement. An "extra courtesy prompt to save the player
from their own glaring stupidity" feature just is a bit too much of a
convenience feature for me (and i'd be bloody annoyed being presented
with it when i know perfectly well what i'm doing).

> To err is human.

As is learning from errors...

> And touching a cockatrice corpse by accident (or most of the other
> things mentioned in my list) is hardly "getting a minor disadvantage".
> It's instant death.

I had a death by picking up a cockatrice corpse after a titan had
disintegrated my gloves. And i was of the opinion that i richly
deserved it _because i hadn't payed attention_. I find it almost too
convenient that the game will ask me whether i really want to pray,
yes/no.

The game at heart assumes that you know what you're doing and lets you
deal with the consequences. An "anti-brain-fart" scroll wouldn't make
the game any better in my opinion.

